{
  "hypothesis": "faults absent from the USGS/INGENIOUS catalogue still force a knickpoint (steepness step) into every channel that crosses them; because a fault is a line, those knickpoints from INDEPENDENT first-order drainages are collinear along the fault strike, so collinearity is the detector and the traces it yields are the submission",
  "mechanism": "Kirby & Whipple (2012) channel steepness + Perron & Royden (2013) chi transform on a priority-flood-filled DEM; per-link least-squares dz/dchi segmentation (Gailleton et al. 2019); one strongest knickpoint per first-order basin; strike chaining of knickpoints from >= 3 distinct basins (offset tolerance 3 px + 3 deg wedge, 36 azimuth bins, NMS by support then residual, principal-axis refit); masked for catalogue ring, playa/closed basin and acquisition striping",
  "named_non_fault_mimic_process": [
    "a strike-parallel LITHOLOGIC CONTACT between resistant and weak units produces the same collinear knickpoint train with no fault (this lane cannot separate the two with DEM alone)",
    "transient incision from a base-level fall propagates as a WAVE up a master drainage and can align knickpoints along a structural grain without recent faulting",
    "PLEISTOCENE/HOLOCENE SURFICIAL EDGES (fan heads, basalt flow margins, pediment break-in-slope) cut channels at slope breaks that mimic fault traces",
    "DEM ACQUISITION STRIPING (flagged: 56 rows) and the coarse 100 m substrate itself"
  ],
